//github.com/vllm-project/vllm/releases/tag/v0.34.0rc1","d":"2026-11-12","p":"2026-11-11","s":["gh_vllm"],"ti":["vendor"],"k":"release","dom":["genai_llm","distributed_cloud"],"ent":[],"eti":[],"ls":{"curriculum":0.9,"research":0.3,"industry":0.8,"awareness":1.2},"sc":1.2,"lh":[],"sum":""},{"t":"dbt-core v2.0.0-dev.44-test-pypi","u":"https://github.com/dbt-labs/dbt/releases/tag/v2.0.0-dev.44-test-pypi","d":"2026-11-12","p":"2026-11-11","s":["gh_dbt"],"ti":["vendor"],"k":"prerelease","dom":["data_engineering","dbms"],"ent":["organisations/amazon-web-services","organisations/databricks","organisations/snowflake","standards/oauth","technologies/bigquery","technologies/clickhouse","technologies/dbt"],"eti":["technologies/dbt"],"ls":{"curriculum":0.2,"research":0.1,"industry":0.3,"awareness":0.5},"sc":0.48,"lh":[],"sum":""}],"total":850,"arxiv_total":681,"arxiv_shown":60,"brief":"---\ndate: 2026-11-12\nweekday: Thursday\ngenerated: 2026-11-12T07:47:36+08:00\nkind: reviewed daily brief\ncovers: 2026-11-12-data.md\n---\n\n# IT brief, Thursday 12 November 2026\n\nAWS released AWS Emulator, a public-domain local emulator of AWS APIs intended for development, CI pipelines and coding agents, and GitHub's Agentic Workflows reached general availability with sandboxing and spending caps. Python lost one of its earliest and most influential contributors, Tim Peters.\n\n## Notable items\n\n[AWS Emulator](https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/11/aws-emulator-now-available/) accepts existing SDK and CLI calls locally, without an AWS account. Services are generated from AWS's published API models, so coverage is meant to grow release by release; unsupported operations return 501 Not Implemented, and a fidelity report compares behaviour with AWS. Each instance keeps its own in-memory state, so parallel test suites do not see each other's resources. The [source](https://github.com/aws/aws-emulator) is under CC0. For cloud and database courses it offers a free sandbox, with the caveat that emulated behaviour is not production behaviour.\n\nGitHub Agentic Workflows, which runs coding agents inside GitHub Actions, is now generally available, [InfoQ reports](https://www.infoq.com/news/2026/11/github-agentic-workflows-ga/). Agents start with read-only permissions in a sandboxed container, proposed changes are scanned and then applied by separate jobs holding write tokens, and runs are capped by default at 1,000 AI credits (US$10). GitHub's FAQ notes that these protections do not extend to direct write permissions or custom jobs an author adds. It is a useful worked example of least privilege and separation of duties for agentic systems.\n\nAmazon DocumentDB, AWS's MongoDB-compatible document database, reached [version 8.0.3](https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/11/amazon-documentdb-8-0-3/): hybrid post-quantum key exchange based on ML-KEM (the NIST lattice-based key-encapsulation standard) for TLS 1.3 when clients support it, wildcard indexes for dynamic schemas, the `$unionWith` aggregation stage, `$lookup` and `$graphLookup` in views, and `dropIndexes` change-stream events. Wildcard indexes and `$unionWith` narrow the feature gap with MongoDB that NoSQL courses often point to.\n\nAlloyDB AI added `ai.classify` and `ai.extract` in [preview](https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/databases/ai-classify-and-ai-extract-functions-in-alloydb-ai/): SQL functions that call Gemini models to label text or turn it into JSON matching a supplied schema, on PostgreSQL 18. LLM calls inside SQL raise questions on cost, determinism and reproducibility that database courses can take up.\n\nGuido van Rossum [announced](https://lwn.net/Articles/1106901/) the death of Tim Peters (1952–2026), author of the Zen of Python (PEP 20), whom he describes as a mentor and language co-designer from Python's early years.\n\nTwo research papers bear on idea generation and enterprise agents. Xu, Zou, Erdogan, Ye, Lu and Zou [propose BVSR](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12184), which generates research proposals with deliberately limited task information and then selects among them using outcomes of earlier verified proposals; across 12 tasks this raised originality and let half of the candidates be discarded while keeping most of the best. Fillies, Fillies, Weichhardt and Wood-Albrecht [describe](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13196) executing BPMN process models as agentic workflows on LangGraph, using process models and knowledge graphs as enterprise context; the abstract reports no quantitative evaluation.\n\n## Also seen\n\n- [Evidence-linked memory](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14320) to reduce memory-induced degradation in LLM agents.\n- Amazon OpenSearch Service [managed cross-Region disaster recovery](https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/11/amazon-opensearch-service-managed-cross-region-disaster-recovery/).\n- Google Cloud's [Professional Agentic Architect certification](https://cloud.google.com/blog/topics/training-certifications/google-cloud-agentic-architect-certification-dont-just-prompt-ai-design-it/).\n- [How a false smoke alarm almost drowned Jane Street's NYC datacenter](https://michael.bacarella.com/2026/11/10/false-smoke-alarm-jane-street-datacenter/).\n\n## Recorded changes\n\n### From vendor and standards sources\n\nThe frame step did not run, for the seventeenth day in a row: the export reports that `exports/daily/2026-11-12-frames.json` was not found (see the frame step's log). Two entity change logs were updated by hand: [Amazon Web Services](../../wiki/entities/organisations/amazon-web-services/index.md), for AWS Emulator, and [GitHub](../../wiki/entities/organisations/github/index.md), for Agentic Workflows' general availability.\n\n### Reported in the news\n\nNone; the frame step did not run.\n"});
